 ],
 "literal": "Will he not remember, O friend? The parrot's / curved beak holding the bright neem fruit, / which, like a sharp, excellent claw of one who inserts a new thread, / resembles a single gold coin of a golden ornament, / in the wilderness of spurge plants on charred ground, which he traversed.",
 "translation": "Will he not remember me, O friend? He who traversed the wilderness of spurge plants on charred ground, where the bright neem fruit held in the curved beak of a parrot resembles a single gold bead of a golden ornament being pierced by the sharp, excellent nail of a jeweler threading a new string.",
 "modern": "தோழியே! கருகிய நிலத்தில் கள்ளிச் செடிகள் நிறைந்த காட்டைத் கடந்து சென்ற நம் தலைவர் என்னை நினைக்க மாட்டாரோ? அங்கே, கிளியின் வளைந்த மூக்கில் கவ்வியிருக்கும் பிரகாசமான வேப்பம்பழமானது, புதிய நூலில் கோப்பதற்காகத் தன் கூர்மையான நகத்தால் பொற்கொல்லன் பிடித்துள்ள தங்க நகையின் ஒரு தங்கக் காசைப் போலத் தோன்றும்.",
 "explanation": "The heroine is speaking to her confidante while grieving the absence of her lover who has departed to earn wealth. He has gone through a harsh desert path, which is the classic setting of the pālai tiṇai. The poem describes the desolate landscape of charred ground and dry spurge plants, but introduces a vivid, delicate image within it. A parrot holding a bright yellow neem fruit in its curved beak is compared to a goldsmith holding a gold bead with a sharp fingernail to thread it. This striking visual contrast suggests that even in the barren desert, such beautiful sights will remind the hero of his beloved's beauty and make him hasten his return.",
